 that were bound to each of  these discrete segments  the data are sort of summarized here  this is probably impossible to really  make much sense out of  but the thing that i'll call your  attention to is the red the red  indicates significant overexpression  in patients that did not recur  and i think that what you can see here  rather easily  is that there's a lot going on in the  tumor the tumor is the the  in the the tumor segments of the cd45  tumor  cd68 tumor and the tumor without  adjacent stroma  have a large number of proteins that are  differentially expressed  whereas in the stroma it's really rather  quiet the only things that show up are  in all of the segments are gcmb and  pdl2 so there's something going on in  the stroma i mean  the tumor excuse me there's there's  information in the tumor that is not  recapitulated in the stroma and for  example i'll show you here cd56  the nk cell marker cd56 in tumor  segments is associated with durable  recurrence-free survival  in this case in cd60a in tumor that's  adjacent to cd68  enrichment so it's significant cd56 is a  significant prognostic indicator  in the tumor segments but is not a  significant prognostic indicator  in the adjacent stromal segments so  there's a lot of information that we're  getting from the tumor  that we're not getting from the strong  we're going to focus in the rest of this  talk actually  on three particular proteins two antigen  presenting cell  protein cd40 and hladr and then the nk  cell marker cd56 as you can see in the  table  all three of these are significantly  associated with  outcome in the tumor segments  but none of them are significantly  associated with outcome  in the stronghold signal so this is  again consistent with the idea  that there's information that we're  getting in the tumor  that's not being obtained if we simply  look at the stromal segments  now the issue that comes up when you  look at these data as well  t4dc56c hlidr  those are are fairly well known um  lymphoid cell markers what's the chance  that when we see these things that are  associated with outcome that we're just  having we're just it's a surrogate view  of tumor infiltrating lymphocytes or  tills so is there really more  information here  than we would get if we had simply  measured tumor infiltrating lymphocytes  well we don't have classical h e stained  tumor infiltrating lymphocyte data from  the fenextrial  but what we do have is very high  resolution digital images from tumors  that are stained with cd45 and cd68  and my colleague jody carter  developed a series of analyses using  halo  instrumentation to measure the number of  cd45  positive cd68 minus cells  to measure the density of these in each  of the tumors so this is essentially  digital tills analysis so we look at  that  we see initially that tumor infiltrating  lymphocytes are in fact prognostic and  triple negative breast cancer they  should be that's been known for a long  time  another question becomes is there  anything other than this going  on and in fact what we see  is that tumor infiltrating lymphocytes  can convey a certain amount of  information  but not the whole story so what i've  presented here  is cd45 positive cells plotted against  the concentration of  hladr each of those dots represents a  single tumor  the red dots represent tumors that  recurred  the green dots represent tumors that did  not  occur okay now if you look  at the lower left hand corner of that  slide what you'll see  is that tumors that have very low level  of  cd45 positive cells and low levels of  hladr  that those patients do very well don't  say those are mostly red those patients  recur okay but if you look  above that if you look at the low till  high  hladr what you'll see is that there is a  population of cell  tumors excuse me that have low level  of tumor infiltrating lymphocytes but  have in fact high levels of tills  of high levels of hladr  and those patients do perfectly well  okay so in general when we look at these  data what we tend to see  is that hladr in the tumor segments  is a more robust prognostic indicator  than total tumor infiltrating  lymphocytes  we looked at this in somewhat more  detail in three dimensions here  using cd40 hladr and cd56  to distribute the tumors again each dot  is a single tumor  green is no recurrence red is  recurrence these are stromal segments  so we've looked at the expression of  these three markers in stromal segments  and as you can see  there's a considerable amount of  heterogeneity within the stroma  but for the most part the tumor samples  that recur  pretty much overlap with the tumor  samples that do not recur  we see a very different situation when  we look in the tumor segments themselves  what we see here is that the tumors that  recur  the red dots here cluster very tightly  with low level abundance of cd40 hladr  and cd56 so the tumors that recur  are uniformly cold for these three  markers  in the tumor segments conversely  the tumors that did not recur exhibit a  great deal of heterogeneity so they're  fairly well spread out  based on these three individual markers  and and distribute very differently  from the tumors that recur  if we look at this in two dimensions we  see something even more striking  in this case i've plotted hladr  expression  against cd56 expression and what you can  see  is that hladr and cd56 are basically  expressed in different subsets of tumors  so tumors that have high level hladr  tend to have very low levels cd56  tumors that have high levels dd56 tend  to have very low level hla dr  so what we're really looking at here is  three different immune landscapes  we've got an immune landscape that that  contains very low level  cd56 and very low level hladr  and by and large those patients don't do  very well at all  we have an additional immune landscape  that contains high-level cd56 and  low-level hladr  those tumors those individual tumors  tend not to recur likewise tumors that  have high-level hla dr  and low-level cd56 10 to recur  so to summarize what i've shown you up  to this point  uh i i think that our data strongly  indicate that the immune cells that are  in intimate contact with the tumor cells  may in fact be more informative than  just looking at total mercedes and  stromal  tumor infiltrated lymphocytes i think  that our data indicates that tumors that  have  high level expression of natural killer  cell markers  are androgen presenting cell markers  have better prognosis  and that seems to be um irrespective of  the presence  or our relative  lack of tumor infiltrating lymphocytes  in those tumors  so we're seeing something that's really  quite different from what we see if we  simply measure stromal  tumor infiltrate lymphocytes and then  finally as i emphasized previously  we're looking at three potentially  distinct immune landscapes  identified by the markers that i've  shown you here  so we have a cold immune landscape those  bases tend to do poorly  we have a cd56 or natural killer cell  enriched  presumed to be a natural killer cell  enriched  landscape and those patients do well and  then we have an  additional immune landscape that's  characterized by high  hladr i can show you the same thing with  cd40 they're both markers for antigen  presenting cells  and tumors that have high level  expression of either or both of those  apc cell markers tend not to recur  so there's multiple immune landscapes  that are  detectable within tumor segments that  are associated with  favorable prognosis in triple negative  breast cancer  none of those are detectable within the  strong signals  i'd like to finish this afternoon by  telling you some  data from a preliminary analysis of a  triple negative breast cancer tissue  microarray this microarray contains 287  patients  and was constructed by the mayo clinic  breast cancer spore and we obtained it  through the good offices of matt getz  this tissue microarray is really quite  different from the fenex samples that  we've been discussing  so the phenx samples were selected for  early stage tumors whereas the tissue  microarray was  essentially drawn from all mayo clinic  triple negative breast cancer patients  so there's no selection for tumor stage  or grade in fact there's no selection  for therapy among these  patients as opposed to the fenex extract  in which all the patients were treated  with the same therapy  so this is a tissue microarray it was  constructed to have two cores from each  tumor  so we've got a maximum of four segments  from each tumor too from each core as  opposed to 24 segments  that we had with the fenex tumor so  there's there's considerably more  um statistical assurance in the  quality of the data from the triple  negative breast cancer  from the from the fenex x trial than it  is from the the  tma  the cores were selected for the tma by a  pathologist who simply went in and  selected for course that had high tumor  density  so this is really quite different from  the selection process that was used from  the fenex trial and these cores are  actually  fairly similar to the tumor without  adjacent  stroma regents of interest that we  selected from fedex x  and then finally the thenxx analyses  were done on  a beta version of the genomics of the  geoex dsp  machine using beta reagents  the tma was analyzed on the commercial  version of the machine using commercial  reagents which involves some  subtle changes in the antibodies that  were used to measure these proteins  so what i'm really telling you is that  there's almost nothing in common between  the fenix egg samples and the tissue  microarray samples other than the fact  that they're both  negative so it's really rather  surprising when you look at the data  this is a plot of  the 39 proteins that were common  to eat to both the phenix x and the  tissue microarray analysis  each spot there each dark spot black  spot  is a single protein and you can see that  there's actually a remarkable  correlation  between the normalized counts for every  protein  in the fenex samples  dsp technology the ability to compare  two different two very very different  sets of tumors  um at the level of normalized uh permian  expression  and get this kind of correlation is is  almost breathtaking  at any rate we went on and we carried  out a series of experiments we measured  the intensity we measured the abundance  of  the the proteins uh using dsp technology  and as you can see here that we got for  the tumors that recurred  in the tissue microarray were very  similar to those that we observed with  the connects x  the tumors that recurred cluster very  tightly with respect to these three  markers these tumors are cold whereas we  look at the tumors that did not recur we  see  essentially the same thing we see a  great deal of heterogeneity  within the tumors that did not recur  and that heterogeneity again reflected  by differences in the expression of  these three markers cda  cd40 hladr and cd56  when we look at this again in two  dimensions you see it's something that's  really quite similar to what we saw  with the finnex x data we're seeing  three different  or a minimum of three different immune  landscapes there's a high hladr  landscape a high cd56 landscape and then  a cold landscape  and those landscapes really differ with  respect to their  prognostic significance the cold tumors  tend to occur  whereas the hladr or low feed cd56 do  not  and the high cd56 likewise  tend to have better prognosis so  to wrap this up a few things i think uh  warrant being said by way of conclusion  i think that the data show you  um in this brief presentation and again  i emphasize that what i've shown you are  mostly highlights  from these studies but i think that what  the data indicate  is that the genomics dsp platform is  really providing a powerful new way for  us to begin to look at the immune  landscape  of breast cancers like not only breast  cancers but other sorts of tumors as  well  i think the most interesting thing that  we're that we're beginning to see  is that there is information within the  tumor compartment  that's going to give us new insight into  immune features that are associated with  therapeutic outcome and breast cancer  i think that we're seeing that there are  multiple immune landscapes i've i've  shown you i believe evidence  for three immune landscapes but  undoubtedly there are other types of  immune landscapes within  the triple negative breast cancer these  immune landscapes appear to be  mutually exclusive that is there are  some tumors  that have high cd56 and they do very  well  there are other tumors that have high  hladr and they also do well  but those are not the same tumors  different immune  landscapes associated with algae and  then finally i think that they overall  indicate the fact that  analysis of protein abundance in a  spatially defined  manner is going to really add a whole  new dimension to vital marker discovery  and to enable us to begin to ask  fundamental questions about the immuno  immunobiology  of breast cancer and the relationship  between immunobiology and therapeutic  outcome  in this case in terms of chemotherapy  but also in terms of immunotherapy as  well  so i'll finish up by expressing my  thanks to the patients who contributed  their samples  to the fennec sex trial to haiki johnshu  who was the  principal investigator on that trial  without him we obviously could not have  gotten any samples torsten nielsen the  university of british columbia  actually initiated these studies which  were funded in part by the national  cancer institute  the breast cancer research foundation  and donna deegan  and then finally i need to express my  profound thanks i noticed ring not only  um doug henerfield and sarah warren and  duran brower who were involved in  experimental design and interpretation  but  specifically to the outstanding genomic  support that even held our hands  through all of these now  um almost 600 tumors that we've analyzed  to date  so i'll stop at that point thank you for  your attention and i'll attempt to  answer any questions that you may have  thank you dr thompson for a very  informative presentation  i have a few questions for you your data  seem to imply  the stromal tumor infiltrating  lymphocytes  may be relatively important  to the outcome of triple negative breast  cancer do you agree with this  interpretation of your data  uh no i i don't agree with that at all i  i did not mean to give you that  impression certainly  we know that stromal tumor infiltrating  lymphocytes are our  prognostic significance in triple  negative breast cancer  and there's a lot of discussion about  whether or not evaluation of stromal  till  should become part of routine clinical  practice so i think that that's a  that's been a very useful way to look  at the immunobiology of triple negative  breast cancer  but i think the reality of it is that  there's a great deal more information  that we can have um above and beyond  just simply looking at stromal tills  we've looked at stromal tumor  infiltrating lymphocytes because that's  what we could do  because we could stain the stroma we  could count the lymphocytes it's very  difficult to count lymphocytes and tumor  by conventional h d it's very difficult  to count macrophages  but the the dsp technology now gives us  the ability to look  at protein markers that are associated  with natural killer cells or  with apc cells  or macrophages and to look at that  within both the stroma  and the tumor so no i'm not saying that  strongholds are not important they  certainly  are important i'm just saying that the  immune cells that  ultimately i think are going to be more  important are the immune cells that are  within the tumor  it's the immune cells that are actively  engaged with the tumor cells  rather than the immune cells that are  out in the stroma  kind of wondering what they're supposed  to do next  thank you along the same lines do you  have any  insights into the mechanism that  underline the difference  with respect to heterogeneity that you  observe in tumor semen  from patients who occur in those who do  not  that that that's almost a version of the  question that i  just answered um we do not at this time  understand why some tumors have high  levels of cd56  other tumors have high levels so hladr  or cd40  obviously that's a very important  fundamental biology question  but we have not yet been able to  tease out the answer  thank you very much interestingly  your date identify a subpopulation of  tumors that are immunologically cold  but did not occur if you look at this  cold tumor to see if there are any other  immunological features  that might be pronounced  so the question of validation uh comes  up when you're looking  at any new technology of this sort um  one attempt that we've made at  validation obviously is to do  immunohistochemistry using antibodies  against  some of these markers  the problem that we've run into in  trying to do that is that  immunohistochemistry is actually  relatively insensitive and many of these  markers that we're looking at  are not high abundance markers dsp is  exquisitely sensitive and so we can see  we can measure things we can quantify  things  and quantify changes in immune proteins  using dsp at a level that's far far  below the lower limits of detection of  immunohistochemistry  so that that method of validation is has  not been successful for us  thus far i will say the following thing  i think that the data that i  presented um in comparing the  fenex tumors to the tissue microarray  tumors  does speak at to one level of validation  the observation that with those two  radically different  tumor subsets that when we look at  the abundance of  a large number of individual proteins in  those two subsets that show that they  correlate very highly  from one to the other i think that that  does speak to the robustness of  technology it doesn't necessarily mean  that the technology is accurate but it  does mean that it's reproducible  so i think that that at this point um  validation is a is a question that that  we're all concerned with  and um we and a number of other  laboratories are  are working on that at this time  thank you very much finally have you  attempted to validate  any of your observations using  orthogonal technologies  um not yet i you know obviously there is  a population  of triple negative breast cancer  patients whose tumors are cold  most of those tumors recur but some of  those cold tumors do not recur it so  presumably there are  features that we can discern within that  subpopulation of  cold tumors that um do not recur  presumably that's  that may be another immune landscape  alternatively it may have nothing  whatsoever to do with the immune system  but  um we're we're we're obviously aware of  that potential question  and it's a subject of ongoing  investigation  so in summary i think that we're  entering  a new field the field of spatial biology  and that we can look  forward to fundamental  new observations on the nature of breast  cancer  the way that breast tumor cells interact  with immune cells  and the consequences of those  interactions  with respect to  therapy ultimately i think contributing  to  the overall goal that we're all working  for and that is to reduce  breast cancer mortality thank you for  your attention  thank you again dr thompson for your  presentation and discussion
 

